window.harnessChunks["rows-963d713eb49860eb.js"]=["<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T23:15:49+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OpenHands/OpenHands\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenHands</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OpenHands/OpenHands/tree/28464621d879e3e9b3ceeae9d70a71d96da6212d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 28464621d879e3e9b3ceeae9d70a71d96da6212d\">2846462 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: the standard product runs an autonomous OpenHands coding agent that executes workspace/tool actions through the first-party Agent Server.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: multiple backends, conversations, child-conversation plumbing and automation dispatch are deployment/routing mechanisms, not an autonomous anti-oscillation/shared-resource coordination function among peer S1 units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the control center exposes operator/runtime controls and settings; no reviewed path gives an autonomous superior agent whole-system resource, priority and commitment authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: confirmation/security/review surfaces constrain or expose operational work, but no distinct autonomous complementary-audit agent is established at this repository boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: schedules, webhooks and external integrations trigger current operations; they do not form a prospective environment-intelligence loop shaping future organizational adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: backend/model/tool/security choices and ultimate task authority remain operator/application configured.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0b75c7fb0539831a89e5ca33f4cf1df430f3550f/assessments/openhands.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0b75c7fb0539831a89e5ca33f4cf1df430f3550f\">0b75c7f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T14:05:51+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Skyvern-AI/skyvern\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Skyvern</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Skyvern-AI/skyvern/tree/f5f3f28a9ce0021a1db24286928a7bff6582a1eb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f5f3f28a9ce0021a1db24286928a7bff6582a1eb\">f5f3f28 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-driven execution of browser tasks against changing web pages, with repeated perception, action selection, tool/browser effects and feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar websites, forms, page navigation, site errors, interaction state and model/tool failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose context-specific browser actions and whether to continue an individual assigned goal in the running model/tool loop. - Decision owner: The model-driven first-party agent at task recursion; host runtime bounds what can execute. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Browser/Playwright layer, ForgeAgent task and step state, retries, tools, configured step/timeout controls, persistence and result capture. - Closure path: Task goal and page evidence → model action proposal → browser tool execution → fresh page/action results → next step or completion decision. - Boundary reachability: The supported local SDK/API invokes the shipped ForgeAgent and executor, not only an example script (README.md; skyvern/forge/agent.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-selected actions change the operational trajectory; deterministic execution limits and browser services are enforcement rather than the task-level actor. - Evidence: skyvern/forge/agent.py; skyvern/forge/agent.py; skyvern/forge/agent.py; README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: In workflow-run-group mode, protect independently running child workflows from concurrent/stale-dispatch overlap and provide a return path once in-flight work settles. - Distinct S1 units: Separate child workflow runs prepared and executed for group items (skyvern/services/workflowrungroupservice.py). - Inter-S1 disturbance: A stale claimed item can still have an executing worker; releasing or launching a later child before the previous in-flight execution stops produces an overlapping/concurrent group run contrary to its serial boundary. - Attenuating coordination relation: Claimed-item fencing, in-flight reconciliation, dispatch tokens, cancellation/settlement checks and ordered claimnextitem gating (skyvern/services/workflowrungroupservice.py; skyvern/services/workflowrungroupservice.py). - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Terminal child notification leads to advanceworkflowrungroup and a next-item claim only after reconciliation (skyvern/services/workflowrungroupservice.py). - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The claimed positive mode specifically guards a demonstrated overlap/interference hazard for independently executing child work, not ordinary ordered workflow blocks or a generic task queue. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent in-flight child execution, stale claim owners and race between cancellation/failure and downstream dispatch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Whether a group item may be dispatched while earlier child execution may still be active. - Decision owner: Deterministic, first-party run-group controller (constructor mode), not the child agent deciding its own browser actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Database claims, status tracking, dispatch tokens and workflow-run executor. - Closure path: Child state/termination → reconciliation and fenced decision → release/claim next unit → changed subsequent workflow-run execution. - Boundary reachability: Run-group service submits, dispatches and advances executable child workflow runs in shipped first-party service mode (skyvern/services/workflowrungroupservice.py; skyvern/services/workflowrungroupservice.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Real inter-run attenuation exists in the packaged mode, but its decisive dispatch right is deterministic first-party code, not an autonomous metacoordinator model. - Evidence: skyvern/services/workflowrungroupservice.py; skyvern/services/workflowrungroupservice.py; skyvern/forge/sdk/executor/backgroundtaskexecutor.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Scope is the optional serial group mode only. The credited S2 interference is limited to active overlapping child runs within the shipped serial run-group mode, not generic task queueing or idempotency outside it.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: ? - Function: Potential operator intervention and run-group current-state supervision, not yet established as whole-system current-control authority at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: In-flight workload, stalled or canceled workflow runs and resource/commitment conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Unresolved: group cancellation and retry dispatch exist, but a system-wide discretionary commitment/resource choice by a legitimate owner has not been reconstructed. - Decision owner: Unresolved; deterministic service owns group lifecycle enforcement, while operators own requested cancellations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Group status API, child status enumeration, cancel group, claim/retry executor. - Closure path: Group cancellation and terminal-event rescheduling are real; a whole-system S3 feedback loop remains unproven. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Service monitoring and deterministic cancellation do not themselves imply S3. - Evidence: skyvern/services/workflowrungroupservice.py; skyvern/services/workflowrungroupservice.py; skyvern/forge/sdk/executor/backgroundtaskexecutor.py. - Basis: unknown - Confidence: low - Caveats: Need supported operator surfaces establishing a whole-current view, discretionary priorities/commitments and returned effects. Do not silently upgrade group scheduling to S3.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: Completion verification is documented in code, but the material independence of the complementary observational and judgment path is not yet settled. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False task completion claims and goal/criterion errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The completion verifier may return complete/terminate/continue based on rescraped page state, but the separately independent audit right is unresolved. - Decision owner: Completion-checking LLM handler and first-party result controller; independence from production agent&#39;s ordinary reporting remains to be demonstrated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Fresh scrape, optional action history and alternative completion LLM handler feature flag. - Closure path: A verifier outcome feeds task continuation/termination, but this alone is not sufficient for S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A second model call or routine validator is not automatically an independent complementary audit. - Evidence: skyvern/forge/agent.py; skyvern/forge/agent.py; skyvern/forge/validationevidencerouter.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: The repository includes workflow/script generation and external page adaptation, but no established external-and-prospective adaptation ownership closure at the system boundary yet. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future site changes, repeat-task automation demands and possible workflow evolution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Not established for prospective portfolio/capability adaptation. - Decision owner: Unresolved; a user designing workflows or generating scripts does not automatically constitute first-party S4 closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workflow service, script-generation subsystem, planner levers and reusable workflow definitions. - Closure path: Generated scripts can potentially become future capability; triggering prospective evaluation and return into deployed capability require more evidence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Short-horizon browser reaction and current-step planning are S1; storing a workflow alone is not S4. - Evidence: skyvern/core/scriptgenerations/generatescript.py; skyvern/services/workflowservice.py; skyvern/services/plannerlevers.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: Organizational credentials, task-level constraints and permissions are present, but ultimate policy/identity decision and closed parent-governed return path are not yet established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Security boundaries, acceptable browsing behavior and organizational identity/policy changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Unresolved at the declared deployment recursion; user-supplied constraints and permissions enforce delegated policy but do not establish an ultimate-policy owner in this review. - Decision owner: Unresolved between parent operator and host configuration/developer authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Organization-scoped permissions, API keys, credential checks, policy/configuration flags. - Closure path: Per-request permission filtering exists; a legitimate ultimate-policy decision changing subsequent operation has not yet been traced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Enforcement of static permissions is not S5 ownership. - Evidence: skyvern/cli/mcptools/org.py; skyvern/forge/sdk/executor/backgroundtaskexecutor.py; skyvern/cli/configcommand.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/408784d2b334447cde682baec295fbc95a589955/assessments/skyvern.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 408784d2b334447cde682baec295fbc95a589955\">408784d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T23:12:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/emcie-co/parlant\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Parlant</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/emcie-co/parlant/tree/ea737442b8ae65854a842542e544fbe7e6144bad\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ea737442b8ae65854a842542e544fbe7e6144bad\">ea73744 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously conduct a controlled customer-facing interaction turn, including context-sensitive reasoning/tool use and response selection. - Disturbance / variety regulated: non-linear user language, changing conversational context, competing relevant rules, workflow state, external tool results and response uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the concrete response/tool action within the context and constraints assembled by the Parlant engine. - Decision owner: the configured model-driven Parlant agent for fluid operation; strict canned mode constrains the available output without removing the standard autonomous mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: contextual matching engine, observations, guidelines, relationships, journeys, tools/retrievers, glossary/variables and composition modes. - Closure path: user input is matched to relevant state/rules/tools, the agent/tool path executes, and the resulting response/event updates the conversation for the next turn. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model-driven agent owns the substantive interaction choice in the standard fluid path; deterministic context engineering constrains rather than replaces that operational decision. - Evidence: README engine diagrams and examples; first-party runtime/docs. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no inter-S1 coordination function is established at the declared single-agent runtime boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting guidelines and journey branches are regulated inside one operational agent, not between distinct S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party right was found that attenuates interaction among multiple distinct Parlant S1 work units. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: guideline exclusions/dependencies, contextual matching and journey transitions. - Closure path: those mechanisms alter one agent&#39;s context/response path; no inter-unit coordination loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the missing requirement is multiple distinct S1 units and their interference, not model autonomy. - Evidence: README relationships/journeys/context-engineering documentation and runtime structure. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-system current-control function is established above the conversational S1 operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current rule relevance, journey state and tool availability are regulated, but as part of producing the current interaction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate owner was found with whole-system authority over resources, commitments, priorities or current organizational constraints. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: contextual matching, guideline relations, journeys, canned-response constraints and tool gating. - Closure path: no distinct S3 control loop separate from S1 was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: “conversational governance” and behavioral control are product semantics; they do not by themselves establish VSM S3 at this boundary. - Evidence: README design goals, diagrams and runtime concepts. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: response explainability and unwanted-behavior prevention were inspected, but these do not create an independent audit judgment loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate reviewer/auditor decision right with corrective feedback into control was found. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OpenTelemetry tracing, guideline match logs, constraints and pre-approved/canned responses. - Closure path: observability records ordinary runtime decisions; no independent audit finding is returned to force correction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: logging/explainability and deterministic guardrails are evidence/enforcement, not a complementary independent auditor. - Evidence: README explainability and design-goal sections. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate external/future intelligence and adaptation loop is established in the standard runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: product/user feedback may motivate developer changes, but that workflow is outside the assessed runtime. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party owner was found that senses external/future distinctions, generates adaptation options and returns a chosen change into present capability/S3. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: external retrievers/tools can fetch current information for S1 tasks; developers can revise guidelines from feedback. - Closure path: no first-party outside-and-then adaptation closure is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: external information use during a customer task and developer maintenance are not S4 by themselves. - Evidence: README tools/retrievers and design goal describing product-feedback-to-implementation workflow. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established at the Parlant runtime boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: developer-authored brand/policy rules constrain behavior but do not constitute an S5 authority process. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy dispute resolution right with authoritative return was found. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: guidelines, agent description, journeys, canned responses and developer configuration. - Closure path: authored constraints feed directly into operational context; no identity-level authority loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy-like content or “on-brand” behavior is not VSM S5 merely because it shapes outputs. - Evidence: README design goals and customization examples. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9f83e41179680c24cbe23a5091abda11fcc221d0/assessments/parlant.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9f83e41179680c24cbe23a5091abda11fcc221d0\">9f83e41 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T22:21:05Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SciPhi-AI/R2R\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">R2R</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SciPhi-AI/R2R/tree/9c5a94d151f90876bd7eb860f300a8fd662dc481\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9c5a94d151f90876bd7eb860f300a8fd662dc481\">9c5a94d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: the agent chooses retrieval/search/reasoning tools and iterates toward research answers.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: no material multi-S1 anti-oscillation/shared-resource coordination path is established at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: service/runtime and research-loop management do not establish a superior whole-system current regulator over multiple operational units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: the first-party critique mechanism can instantiate a distinct second-opinion/audit relation: a specialized reasoning call reviews the accumulated operational reasoning for flaws and alternative interpretations, then returns findings to the main agent. It is optional/not default and invoked within the research configuration, so it is composable rather than autonomous out of the box.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: web search/retrieval gather information for the current research task. They do not establish a separate prospective environmental-intelligence function that changes future organizational posture.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: prompts, tools, model selection and ultimate goals remain parent/configuration owned.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ae8749aa005ea42801ed86e32476f5c417ecd9a0/assessments/r2r.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ae8749aa005ea42801ed86e32476f5c417ecd9a0\">ae8749a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T03:18:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SalesforceAIResearch/AgentLite\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentLite</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SalesforceAIResearch/AgentLite/tree/b173239a652eea560e57c6fe46b0c0af7c4f3578\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b173239a652eea560e57c6fe46b0c0af7c4f3578\">b173239 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: workers run model-driven action loops over delegated tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: manager selection, delegation and fallback do not implement anti-oscillation/shared-resource regulation among independently interacting S1 units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the manager lacks evidenced whole-system resource, priority and accountability authority with a closed superior feedback loop; the class name alone is insufficient.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: no distinct first-party reviewer/evaluator independently checks operational claims against evidence and feeds corrective findings back into the running organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: no first-party prospective environment/adaptation function is established at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: goals, roles, team membership and ultimate policy are application supplied.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c6f4922f94d64503565f75530e237dac6540e155/assessments/agentlite.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c6f4922f94d64503565f75530e237dac6540e155\">c6f4922 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T21:45:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dyabel/AnyTool\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AnyTool</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dyabel/AnyTool/tree/6202212ce45329282cf812ae48427f8765cf164c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6202212ce45329282cf812ae48427f8765cf164c\">6202212 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform the query-specific transformation from user goal and observed API/tool environment into selected tool calls and a task result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous natural-language requests, an extensive API taxonomy, changing tool results, malformed calls and unsuccessful answers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-selected categories, tools and APIs, followed by a model/tool action sequence that may be revised from tool/error observations. - Decision owner: the first-party wired model-backed MainSearchAgent, CategoryAgent, ToolAgent and downstream solver; external inference provider serves decisions but does not itself own the harness workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: API database functions, Python threads/semaphore, argument limits, schema/function dispatch, RapidAPI wrapper, JSON state and fixed iteration caps. - Closure path: chosen APIs enter the shared pool, the solver attempts the query, execution/solution feedback is returned to search-agent messages, and a new selection or answer follows. - Boundary reachability: scripts/main.py instantiates and runs these actors and solver as its ordinary documented entry point; no separate dogfood actor is necessary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed decision makers leaves only a fixed taxonomy/database and execution shell, not the discretionary API selection and iterative solution. - Evidence: scripts/main.py (MainSearchAgent.assignmain, ToolAgent.toolsearch, main main solve/refind loop); anytool/dfsgt.py; README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for a source-wired research-time agent loop. - Caveats: no external model/tool invocation was run; real-world success/reliability is not established.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate simultaneous tool-search cells&#39; interference over one finite shared pool of selected APIs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: separately executing ToolAgent cells can select the same API or contend for the common max-API resource, producing duplicate selection, pool overflow or premature sufficiency decisions. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently launched tool-search cells below one or more category agents; each searches a different assigned subset of the API taxonomy. - Inter-S1 disturbance: duplicate selected APIs and competing additions against globalapilist/maxapinumber, with a shared stop criterion. - Attenuating coordination relation: addapisintoapipool checks pool capacity, deduplicates APIs, serializes the tool-call insertion through counterlock, and rechecks whether the common pool can solve the query. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: an insertion result reports API count/sufficiency/error to the originating Tool Agent; shared stop and refind feedback constrain further tool-agent selection. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the cited code directly guards a concrete duplicate/over-capacity collision among concurrent API producers. Merely spawning category agents or listing a tree would not suffice. - Decisive decision or feedback right: whether to accept each candidate API into the common bounded pool and return a capacity/sufficiency signal to the search cells. - Decision owner: the shipped deterministic shared-pool coordination code; no agent-owned discretion over cross-cell conflict resolution is evidenced. Autonomous agents choose APIs but do not revise the S2 deduplication/locking rule. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Python Lock, semaphore, pool and count variables, model-based sufficiency check and per-agent messages. - Closure path: accepted/denied/duplicate selection changes shared capacity and reported feedback; search cells subsequently select more APIs or stop. - Boundary reachability: the coordination function is invoked by ToolAgent.toolsearch in the default threaded runner; it is not an unused generic extension point. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the cross-cell collision rule remains the same if the model selector changes; the first-party S2-specific constructor/runtime primitive exists, but no autonomous coordinator owns its policy or correction discretion. - Evidence: scripts/main.py (ToolAgent.addapisintoapipool, toolsearch, counterlock, sem, stop). - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; scope is API-pool interference, not arbitrary inter-agent negotiation. - Caveats: the documented locking protects the addition path; it is not a proof of race-free behavior for every global read, and this narrow coordination is not generalized across separate user-query runs.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no established whole-system current-control function above the task&#39;s routing and global resource enforcement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the task can run out of tokens/APIs or need replanning, but fixed bounds and resuming a search cell do not establish whole-current S3 regulation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party whole-system discretionary allocation/commitment decision beyond per-query category/tool search and hard threshold enforcement was located. - Decision owner: initial model category selection and fixed Python orchestrator logic; developer sets limits; no qualifying S3 owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxapinumber, totaltokens, stop, thread/semaphore orchestration and solver/refind controls. - Closure path: these mechanisms constrain a query&#39;s search or retry; they do not close separate current enterprise/whole-system accountability, resource bargaining or intervention. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main agent chooses search branches for the query, but it is not shown to own a distinct whole-system resource/commitment decision informed by an aggregate current operational view. - Evidence: scripts/main.py (MainSearchAgent, pool checks and main loop); arguments.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this is the single-query standard-distribution boundary, not a claim about all possible deployments.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: plausible in-run challenge of solution claims, with unresolved independence and complementary-access strength. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a solver may report a plausible final answer despite incomplete or invalid API execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: checksolvedtoolbench obtains several ToolBench evaluator verdicts and returns a majority pass/fail plus reason that can trigger a fresh API search; the code establishes a challenge path but does not establish that the evaluator independently accesses ground truth rather than the solver&#39;s own report. - Decision owner: model-backed ToolBench evaluator makes the judgment; Python aggregates outcomes and resumes search. Whether this is sufficiently independent audit ownership at the declared recursion remains unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stored solution transcript, ToolBench conversion, computepassrate and evaluator prompts, a three-call pool, majority vote and reason forwarding. - Closure path: an unsuccessful external evaluation sends failedreason into resumesearch, may prune prior APIs and leads to a subsequent solver attempt. This corrective return is source-wired, conditional on the evaluator verdict. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model evaluator judges the candidate answer, but the independence from the ordinary operational answer claim and materially different access to reality cannot be firmly established from its inputs/prompted status check alone. - Evidence: anytool/verifier.py (checksolvedtoolbench); anytool/checksolved.py (computepassrate, answer/available-tools inputs); scripts/main.py (check result/refind path). - Basis: structural for the evaluator/return, unknown for independent complementary access. - Confidence: medium for existence of a separate challenge, low for positive S3 ownership. - Caveats: benchmark-oriented correctness evaluation may be an adjacent research measurement; the reviewed source does wire it into the default task loop, so a blanket no-path claim would also be premature.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective environmental adaptation function established for continuing harness capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool failures and unsolved current queries drive within-query refind, not a future-oriented capability change across runs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no actor is wired to decide future tool inventory, strategy or platform adaptation from external trend evidence. - Decision owner: model agents revise the current API selection; developer and static configuration determine later deployments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: saved run outputs, API database retrieval, refind prompts, and benchmark score files. - Closure path: within-query retry changes the current solution path but no returned externally/prospectively grounded adaptation into later installed capability is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: solving the current query again is operational correction, not outside-and-then intelligence. - Evidence: scripts/main.py (refind loop and output persistence); README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: nothing here rules out an externally built S4 around the tool.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision and authoritative return path established at the task-solving organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permitted API count, provider keys and task completion criteria are startup constraints, not unresolved organizational identity/policy matters. - Decisive decision or feedback right: configuration and prompts are supplied by developers/operator; no first-party policy-level deliberation/ratification path is wired. - Decision owner: external developer/operator for constants and credentials; no qualifying first-party or parent-governed S5 closure established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: arguments.py, configexample.py, static agent prompts, API whitelist, stop and timeout limits. - Closure path: static configuration conditions operation, but no identity-level issue reaches legitimate ultimate authority and returns as a decision to govern subsequent execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: changing a command-line limit or selecting an API is routine operations/configuration, not ultimate-policy choice. - Evidence: arguments.py; configexample.py; anytool/prompttemplate.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: OSS maintainers have project-level decisions, but repository maintenance is outside the runtime recursion and cannot donate S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/204555bb88245a2610dfd226b5bcd2a052166282/assessments/an/anytool.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 204555bb88245a2610dfd226b5bcd2a052166282\">204555b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T23:04:51+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MinorJerry/WebVoyager\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WebVoyager</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MinorJerry/WebVoyager/tree/5a7896738c10bfb8b9edccce6bb0e0411f8ae569\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5a7896738c10bfb8b9edccce6bb0e0411f8ae569\">5a78967 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing browser navigation and information retrieval to satisfy an incoming web task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dynamic page content, clickable-element labels, web forms, navigation paths, downloads, dialog state, selector failures and incomplete answers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model chooses click/type/scroll/goback/google/wait/answer, selecting element labels/content and revising its next action from screenshots/trees and error feedback. - Decision owner: first-party model-backed step decision loop in run.py; the externally hosted model supplies text/vision inferences but the repository wires their decisions into executable browser actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Selenium ChromeDriver, visual set-of-mark tags, accessibility-tree parser, model completion API wrapper, action parser, fixed maxiter and exception handling. - Closure path: task + live screenshot/tree → model action → browser mutation and new observation/warning → next model action or final answer. - Boundary reachability: run.sh/documented root run.py enters this while-loop for each task, executes selected actions and re-observes the browser before the next choice. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party decision path uses model-chosen actions and returns environment feedback; the Selenium routines have no independent discretion over which site/element to visit. - Evidence: run.py (main observation/model/action loop, execaction, error branch); utils.py (visual observation and action parsing). - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for wired decision/feedback path; not empirical success. - Caveats: browser actions are not safety-reviewed; the code was read, not run on any real site.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no inter-S1 interference attenuation at the selected single-task agent boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model-selected browser actions can affect the current page, but no two distinct simultaneously operating S1 cells or conflicting resource claims are established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: static per-task loop sequences one model action and one Selenium execution; it does not negotiate or arbitrate a collision among independent operational cells. - Decision owner: one model-driven browser task cell and fixed Python action dispatcher. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: iteration counter, per-task driver initialization/quit, browser window switching, screenshot element labels and retry messages. - Closure path: a selected action changes the same task&#39;s browser; there is no separate conflict-resolution feedback to another operational unit. - Boundary reachability: for taskid in range(len(tasks)) processes task episodes in series, each using one drivertask and one action-at-a-time loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: one agent&#39;s self-correction and sequential use of the browser is S1 operation, not S2 coordination among separately autonomous units. - Evidence: run.py (main task loop, while it &lt; args.maxiter, action branches, drivertask.quit). - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for documented runner; alternative external multi-instance deployments are outside scope. - Caveats: a system operator can launch separate processes, but no first-party coordination relation among them is shown.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated whole-service current control or commitment/resource bargaining over several operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: browser tasks may be costly, slow, unreliable or blocked, but the harness uses fixed iteration/model retry thresholds and static CLI options. - Decisive decision or feedback right: maxiter and API retry caps terminate or retry a single task; no actor receives a whole-system current view and chooses cross-task resource/priority/commitment interventions. - Decision owner: operator-defined CLI limits and deterministic Python branches, not autonomous or substantive deterministic S3 authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxiter, token/cost logging, API retries, time.sleep, task directory output and ChromeDriver shutdown. - Closure path: limits stop individual tasks, error strings prompt S1 retry; no global performance control decision returns to running subordinate cells. - Boundary reachability: limits run in the published source, but they enforce startup bounds rather than a separate whole-current managerial function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an action controller and per-task retry mechanism do not constitute whole-organizational resource balancing just because they orchestrate a loop. - Evidence: run.py (main args, loop, callgpt4vapi retry cap). - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: no external multi-task scheduler or hosted system-management console is inside the assessed repository boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no in-run independent complementary audit of browser task completion claims. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a model can report an incorrect answer or wrong page, but the shipped task loop does not cross-check the report through independent sampled ground truth. - Decisive decision or feedback right: screenshots, DOM-derived labels and download/PDF Assistant results are ordinary task observations; the answer action ends the task without an independent verification gate. - Decision owner: ordinary S1 model and deterministic environment observation; evaluation/autoeval.py is a separate offline benchmark artifact rather than an in-run audit owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task screenshots, saved interaction messages, per-task logs, PDF assistant information extraction and benchmark evaluation directory. - Closure path: an error/warning can enter the next model step; there is no separate audit verdict to whole-system control capable of overturning a declared completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: even if the PDF Assistant is model-powered, it returns requested task content, not a complementary operational audit; an offline benchmark judge cannot be promoted to S3 inside the runtime. - Evidence: run.py (answer branch, PDF handling, logging); evaluation/autoeval.py (adjacent evaluator). - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for distribution boundary. - Caveats: whether an external benchmark evaluates transcripts has no bearing on runtime audit ownership without an actual return-to-control path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective external environment intelligence updating the harness&#39;s continuing operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: websites change across browser actions, but the agent adapts its current navigation by taking a fresh screenshot/tree after each action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model chooses the next web action for the same task; it does not build a future web ecosystem model, compare prospective adaptation options or update enduring harness strategy. - Decision owner: task-level model for navigation, developer/operator for future prompts, browser/model deployment and code revisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: message history, screenshot clipping, static prompt, max iterations, saved examples and benchmark scripts. - Closure path: new web observation returns to current S1 action; no S4-to-S3 assessed future option returns to current installation/capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reactive re-navigation after an external event is operational correction and does not establish outside-and-then intelligence. - Evidence: run.py (formatmsg, main messages/loop); prompts.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: web browsing could surface external future information for a user task; the reviewed harness does not itself perform prospective organizational adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate ultimate-policy/identity authority exercised inside the shipped task runner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: allowed sites, credentials and browser settings can affect safety and task scope, but these are supplied before run and not policy questions resolved by the harness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user/operator supplies testfile, API key, model, Chrome settings and iteration cap; the model chooses within-task browser actions without ultimate-governance escalation. - Decision owner: external operator/developer and task model for local choices; no recognized first-party policy owner with parent decision return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static SYSTEMPROMPT, CLI configuration, iteration cap, browser preferences and API call limits. - Closure path: startup configuration conditions task execution, but there is no path for an identity or ultimate-policy issue to reach ratifying authority and return as a governing change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a system prompt and launch-time constraints do not constitute policy identity or legitimate S5 decision rights. - Evidence: run.py (argument setup and runner); prompts.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: user authorization for real web transactions and repository owner governance are separate concerns outside this script&#39;s claimed autonomy.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f288c21d3ecdd9557c1403df4ea4f28f9800c03f/assessments/we/webvoyager.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f288c21d3ecdd9557c1403df4ea4f28f9800c03f\">f288c21 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T22:18:13+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xingyaoww/code-act\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CodeAct</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xingyaoww/code-act/tree/d607f56c9cfe9e8632ebaf65dcaf2b4b7fe1c6f8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d607f56c9cfe9e8632ebaf65dcaf2b4b7fe1c6f8\">d607f56 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform an open-ended user request into appropriate code-execution actions and a subsequent answer at the task boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable natural-language questions, available Python state, software/tool/library outcomes, exceptions and intermediate computed observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model selects executable Python and the subsequent code/answer sequence in response to interpreter output, within a configured maximum of code executions. - Decision owner: first-party model-backed Agent calling Generator; model service returns content but does not own the harness&#39;s code-execute-and-observe workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: &lt;execute&gt; parser, ClientJupyterKernel, Jupyter execution API, conversation message list, execution-count limit, Docker/Kubernetes gateway. - Closure path: user task → generated code → first-party API execution → observed text/error → new model message → revised code or final response. - Boundary reachability: Agent.run drives the documented Python CLI; Agent.handleusermessage calls handleexecution, which calls the session executor and appends its returned output for the next generation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model&#39;s discretionary code/action choice leaves a fixed execution transport; code selection and continuation are model driven, while safety/isolation primitives are deterministic. - Evidence: scripts/chat/demo.py (Agent.handleusermessage, handleexecution, Generator.generate); README.md CodeAct interaction description. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source-wired feedback/continuation. - Caveats: upstream model quality and safety of generated code are unverified; local code execution was deliberately not run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate code-execution state interference between distinct simultaneously served chat-session operational cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two independent conversations could otherwise overwrite variables, packages, execution state or temporary files when sharing one long-lived Python interpreter. - Distinct S1 units: each separately identified conversation runs a user-facing code-action cell, potentially concurrently through the common Tornado execution API. - Inter-S1 disturbance: one session&#39;s mutable interpreter global state or installed package changes could pollute another session&#39;s calculations/outputs if both shared a single kernel. - Attenuating coordination relation: ExecuteHandler keys a per-session convidtokernel map on convid; an unknown conversation instantiates its own Docker container or Kubernetes pod/gateway plus kernel, while subsequent calls reuse only that session&#39;s kernel. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: code execution returns results from the correct session-specific kernel, and later actions use that session&#39;s state without another conversation&#39;s changes; expired sessions are removed, requiring new isolated state. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: separate stateful execution sandboxes address a specific destructive cross-cell state collision; merely forwarding a request or tagging a message would not insulate mutable interpreter state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine session-to-kernel ownership and enforce separation; it is a deterministic per-conversation binding, not an autonomous negotiation between session agents. - Decision owner: shipped ExecuteHandler plus JupyterGatewayDocker or JupyterGatewayKubernetes session allocation mechanism; no separate agent chooses/revises the isolation rule. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session map, per-conversation Docker container/pod creation, Jupyter kernel initialization, unique names, timeouts, CPU/memory quotas and gateway teardown. - Closure path: convid on each request → select/create isolated environment → execute only in its kernel → return that result to the originating agent&#39;s dialogue. - Boundary reachability: default Docker branch in api.py runs JupyterGatewayDocker; USEKUBERNETES=1 selects a shipped Kubernetes backend. The README documents per-chat container execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: cell-specific model agents do task-level work but deterministic server code owns the collision-attenuating session binding; hence C rather than A. - Evidence: scripts/chat/codeexecution/api.py (ExecuteHandler.post, convidtokernel, cleanupkernels); scripts/chat/codeexecution/jupyter.py (JupyterGatewayDocker.enter, JupyterGatewayKubernetes.enter). - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for intended isolation; actual isolation under running containers was not tested. - Caveats: this is narrow per-session interpreter-state coordination, not arbitrary peer-agent workflow negotiation or multi-tenant security certification.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no independently evidenced whole-service current resource/commitment regulation beyond fixed session lifecycle and quotas. - Disturbance / variety regulated: runaway code executions or stale kernels may occupy CPU/memory, but the code applies static per-container limits and fixed idle cleanup rather than a whole-system discretionary resource decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a fixed nmaxexecutions truncates one task turn, and 10-minute idle cleanup tears down old containers; no aggregate resource/commitment negotiation with a global view is shown. - Decision owner: Python constants and startup operator, not a distinct operational S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CPU/memory limits, cleanupkernels, PeriodicCallback, execution counter and deployment configuration. - Closure path: stale session cleanup or local execution limit ends that cell, without demonstrated whole-system resource bargaining/accountability across active sessions. - Boundary reachability: session limits are shipped and callable, but this does not establish higher-order S3 regulation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a fixed cleanup rule can enforce a bound without model-owned or substantive deterministic whole-current control decisions. - Evidence: scripts/chat/codeexecution/api.py (cleanupkernels, timer); scripts/chat/codeexecution/jupyter.py (resource constraints); scripts/chat/demo.py (turn limit). - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a separately operated Kubernetes cluster may have a scheduler/control plane, but external orchestration authority cannot be credited to CodeAct.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no complementary inspection of operational assertions independent of the ordinary model-to-interpreter loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code may compute a wrong answer or fail; raw interpreter output is visible, but visibility to the originating agent is part of its normal execution path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: execution output is returned as a normal user-role observation to the same agent; no separately appointed checker can challenge its conclusions with additional reality access. - Decision owner: ordinary session agent and deterministic Jupyter output transport. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Jupyter output, saved dialogue JSON, timeout/errors and optional benchmark evaluation outside the active service. - Closure path: output directly informs next model action or final answer; no independent audit finding returns to an S3 owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model self-correction is operational feedback, and the existence of scripts/eval/ tests does not itself wire an S3 channel into the documented live service. - Evidence: scripts/chat/demo.py (handleusermessage, save); scripts/chat/codeexecution/api.py (ExecuteHandler). - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: externally configured evaluation could add an audit, but it is not part of this first-party runtime boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no sustained externally/prospectively oriented adaptation of the CodeAct operating capability at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an agent can investigate new information via generated code in response to one task, but this remains within-request operational variety. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the agent chooses next code based on current interpreter output; it does not model anticipated environment changes, develop future options and return them through current-control assessment. - Decision owner: operational model for task actions; future model/prompt releases are developer-controlled and outside the runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: conversation history, persisted JSON, model weights produced in separate training tracks and deployment choice. - Closure path: current tool feedback influences current or later messages in the same session, not a prospective adaptation option closing into the service&#39;s installed capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-led programming and observations are agentic, but generic planning, chat memory and iterative task correction alone do not meet S4&#39;s future/outside test. - Evidence: scripts/chat/demo.py (Agent.messages, execution loop, save); README.md (separate model training/deployment). - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for absence in shipped chat runtime. - Caveats: the agent could be asked about the future by a user, but ordinary task response does not confer S4 organizational authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no ultimate identity/policy deliberation or legitimate ratification path within the runtime CodeAct session organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code permissions and model deployment involve risks, but runtime instructions and static container bounds alone are not identity-governing decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the user/operator picks model endpoint and runtime deployment, while the assistant chooses task-level code; no first-party mechanism elevates an ultimate-policy conflict to a legitimate parent and returns an authoritative ruling. - Decision owner: external operator and repository maintainers, not a first-party ACE-like or CodeAct policy council. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static SYSTEMMESSAGE, execution cap, container quotas, launch settings and infrastructure defaults. - Closure path: fixed task limits and prompts shape code actions without any ratified change of identity/policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model instruction, static policy text or Docker boundary is not proof of legitimate ultimate-policy ownership. - Evidence: scripts/chat/demo.py (SYSTEMMESSAGE, args); scripts/chat/codeexecution/jupyter.py (static resource limits); README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: upstream community governance is beyond this assessed runtime, not an inferred S5(P) feedback route.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c7ab7bf3f431421b48eee4698b7459e7c75ae984/assessments/co/codeact.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c7ab7bf3f431421b48eee4698b7459e7c75ae984\">c7ab7bf &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T21:48:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/google-deepmind/concordia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Concordia</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/google-deepmind/concordia/tree/3e30a207bb2b75f9cf837fa6b657c466ca450765\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3e30a207bb2b75f9cf837fa6b657c466ca450765\">3e30a20 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. Player entities autonomously choose putative actions within the simulated domain. Their decisions are the operational transformations the harness exists to exercise.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: C. Concordia supplies an explicit cross-player interaction membrane: the sequential engine allows one entity to act at a time when order matters, while both engine forms route putative actions through common Game Master resolution before the resulting event becomes subsequent shared observation. This closes ordering/interaction interference across multiple S1s rather than merely forwarding messages. The concrete scenario, Game Master components and action semantics remain constructor-supplied, so the organizational coordination path is composable rather than an out-of-box autonomous S2.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: —. Game Master event resolution is authoritative over the simulated world&#39;s causality, but that is the ordinary environment-resolution path. The reviewed repository does not separately establish a whole-organization current view plus authority over shared organizational resources, commitments, priorities or constraints. The historical C promoted environment/world-state control into S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Event resolution and logging are part of the normal simulation path, not materially complementary access to operational reality.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. Running social simulations or choosing future simulated events does not itself create an outside-and-then intelligence loop that adapts the system-in-focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. Scenario premises, Game Master rules and component configuration carry developer-authored policy, but no first-party runtime path was established in which identity/ultimate-policy tension reaches legitimate ultimate authority and returns as changed organizational policy. The historical C therefore does not survive the function-first S5 test.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/911ebc94216226df3120d8a5762944319ae24529/assessments/concordia.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 911ebc94216226df3120d8a5762944319ae24529\">911ebc9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T20:58:46+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/VRSEN/agency-swarm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agency Swarm</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/VRSEN/agency-swarm/tree/853d37b9b243edc5dfe2ff2d2d4658aa8ec9a750\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 853d37b9b243edc5dfe2ff2d2d4658aa8ec9a750\">853d37b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: participating agents directly perform bounded operational work toward the agency&#39;s purpose using model reasoning and tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific user input, tool/environment observations, model feedback, and local execution outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next action/tool and adapt the local trajectory from returned observations. - Decision owner: the agent actor in the standard autonomous run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registries, model/runtime adapters, guardrails, persistence, context, and per-agent tool-concurrency machinery. - Closure path: observation/tool result returns to the agent loop and changes subsequent model/tool action. - Why agent-owned: first-party documentation explicitly describes agents as autonomous goal-driven actors that choose their next action and self-correct from feedback. - Evidence: agent overview at the pinned revision. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: developer-authored instructions and available tools bound the local action space but do not remove the agent&#39;s operational discretion inside that boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: —. - Function finding: multiple agents can coexist, communicate, delegate, and share state, but the reviewed standard distribution does not supply a material first-party S2-specific path that regulates a concrete inter-S1 interference/conflict/oscillation. - Distinct S1 units: multiple autonomous agents may be configured in one Agency. - Inter-S1 disturbance: no built-in disturbance/regulation pair was established. The documentation shows delegation, handoff, shared state, and developer-defined message contracts rather than a first-party anti-oscillation or conflict-resolution responsibility. - Coordination relation: none established beyond generic authored communication/validation primitives. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an S2 function. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: directional communicationflows, SendMessage, handoffs, Agency Context, and optional input/output guardrails. - Closure path: these mechanisms do close ordinary delegation and validation loops, but no S2-specific closure was established. - Why this is not C: SendMessage and handoffs route/delegate work; shared context is generic state; guardrail functions are developer-authored validation hooks. The developer must supply both the organizational disturbance and the coordination policy. ToolConcurrencyManager cannot supply the missing inter-S1 witness because its implementation explicitly manages concurrency for one agent instance. - Evidence: communication-flows docs; concurrency.py; Agency Context docs; guardrail docs/tests. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a particular application can build S2 on these primitives; that composed application would require its own system-in-focus assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: —. - Function finding: no first-party whole-system current-control authority was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not applicable as an S3 mapping; current task delegation and validation remain operational execution support. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no built-in actor is shown maintaining a whole-Agency current view and exercising authority over shared resources, commitments, priorities, constraints, accountability, synergy, or intervention on behalf of the whole. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: orchestrator-worker delegation, shared context, guardrails, per-agent tool serialization, run limits, and runtime state. - Closure path: delegation returns specialist results to the caller and guardrails can alter/retry an individual trajectory, but neither path closes S3 current regulation for the Agency as a whole. - Why not agent-owned / constructor-owned: an orchestrator compiles delegated work, but task allocation and result integration are not S3 without whole-system current authority. Runtime constraints enforce developer-selected limits rather than owning the organizational decision. - Evidence: communication-flows docs; concurrency implementation; guardrail docs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: application code can implement a manager with S3 rights, but framework expressiveness alone does not justify C.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: —. - Claim being audited: ordinary messages and final outputs may be validated by configured guardrail functions. - Ordinary reporting path: the same agent input/output and inter-agent message path used for normal production work. - Complementary access path: none established. Guardrails inspect the ordinary candidate input/output supplied to the runtime; the framework does not provide a separate path with materially different access to operational reality. - Independence boundary: not established by the framework. A developer may call another evaluator agent from a guardrail, but that is application composition and is not a supplied independent audit channel. - Decisive audit judgment / owner: developer-supplied guardrail code or whatever application-defined evaluator it invokes; no first-party S3 organizational owner/path is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tripwires, persisted guardrail feedback, retry, strict abort, and suppression of in-flight subordinate output. - Closure path: guardrail findings can block or feed ordinary retry guidance back into the same operational trajectory; this is routine validation closure, not complementary audit closure. - Evidence: input/output guardrail docs and integration tests. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a separately designed independent evaluator using these hooks could become S3 in a different composed system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - Function finding: no first-party external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop was established. - External distinction: agents can perceive current tools/APIs/user context, but this is ordinary operational sensing. - Future/prospective distinction: no separate future-oriented environmental model owned by the harness was established. - Adaptation option generated: none at organizational capability level. - Path back into current capability / S3: none established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting mechanisms: model planning, context, memory/persistence, external tools, and developer configuration. - Closure path: not applicable for a positive S4 mapping. - Evidence: agent and agency documentation reviewed at the pinned revision. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: current-task planning or self-correction is not S4 by itself.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: —. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision path is supplied for the Agency as system-in-focus. - Ultimate authority: developers/operators author instructions, communication topology, tools, guardrails, and other configuration before or around runs; that authorship is not itself a first-party S5 closure loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party runtime path was established by which an identity/ultimate-policy issue reaches legitimate ultimate authority and an authoritative decision returns to govern subsequent Agency operation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared instructions, agent instructions, topology configuration, guardrails, and runtime enforcement. - Return-to-operation path: no S5-specific return path established. - Why not C or P: static/configurable policy surfaces can constrain operations but do not themselves instantiate S5. Generic human ability to configure, approve, interrupt, or operate the framework does not establish a function-specific parent-governed S5 mode. - Evidence: Agency and agent configuration docs plus guardrail behavior. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a composed organization may retain S5 at a human parent recursion, but that requires evidence for that organization rather than inference from framework configurability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9fc91896ffb9ab0e42f2be118c5545d11ccb8bd2/assessments/agency-swarm.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9fc91896ffb9ab0e42f2be118c5545d11ccb8bd2\">9fc9189 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T04:01:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OpenAgentsInc/openagents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenAgents</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OpenAgentsInc/openagents/tree/a2de475d2ee3a2f00865944c79c15600c1ada45c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a2de475d2ee3a2f00865944c79c15600c1ada45c\">a2de475 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: connected coding-agent engines autonomously execute bounded model/tool work inside the standard product.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: parent/child topology, queues, cancellation and supervised delegation organize work but do not establish a standard autonomous anti-oscillation/shared-resource coordination decision right among peer S1 units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: whole-environment supervision is explicitly an operator control surface. No current autonomous agent owns the superior resource/priority/commitment regulator role.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: OpenAgents has substantial evidence and assurance infrastructure, but the previously autonomous-looking assurance swarm execution path is explicitly retired/deleted at this pinned revision. Current review, receipts and QA projections support human/verifier judgment rather than a standard autonomous complementary-audit agent.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: research, Fast Follow and network/product-roadmap material do not establish a standard runtime S4 decision right; several broader directions explicitly require new owner admission before they gain product authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: the product deliberately retains ultimate goals, authority, private boundaries and result acceptance with the owner/user.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c62c112b9468bc60e2a63b92623b0b69fd6248a6/assessments/openagents.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c62c112b9468bc60e2a63b92623b0b69fd6248a6\">c62c112 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T03:12:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CrewAI</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI/tree/894898f84c4ac0a89f24bf7bee6c381eb0e67f51\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 894898f84c4ac0a89f24bf7bee6c381eb0e67f51\">894898f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: role agents autonomously execute bounded tasks with tools and context.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: delegation, sequential task execution and optional hierarchical assignment do not evidence an autonomous anti-oscillation/shared-resource coordination decision right among independently interacting S1 units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the optional manager delegates configured tasks, while rate limits, planning, callbacks and checkpoints remain application/runtime controls. No reviewed OSS path gives the manager a whole-system current regulator&#39;s authority over priorities, resources and commitments with closed feedback.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: task guardrails/evaluators do not establish an independent standard runtime audit channel, and the named OSS hallucination guardrail is explicitly a no-op by default.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: Crew planning and knowledge/research facilities serve current task execution; no distinct prospective external-intelligence loop feeding future organizational adaptation is established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: roles, goals, process choice, guardrails, security settings and ultimate task policy remain developer/parent supplied.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ff74c380425fb7c8a9cee512289cdb428d74dbb1/assessments/crewai.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ff74c380425fb7c8a9cee512289cdb428d74dbb1\">ff74c38 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T03:18:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/myshell-ai/AIlice\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AIlice</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/myshell-ai/AIlice/tree/63a22105cdf8c0d297af332010f46e4153bbc1ec\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 63a22105cdf8c0d297af332010f46e4153bbc1ec\">63a2210 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: processors autonomously choose tools/subtasks and produce task outcomes inside their delegated scopes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: IACT parent-child delegation and result integration do not establish peer anti-oscillation or shared-resource conflict regulation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the root processor can decompose and call children, but no reviewed mechanism gives it whole-system resource, priority and accountability authority beyond ordinary task delegation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: fault handling and child result integration stay on the operational path; no distinct independent reviewer/auditor compares claims against evidence and returns corrective findings.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: web/tool use and self-expansion claims remain current-task capabilities at this revision; no separate prospective environmental intelligence loop shapes future organizational posture.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: prompts, modules, permissions and ultimate goals remain parent/runtime supplied.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c6f4922f94d64503565f75530e237dac6540e155/assessments/ailice.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c6f4922f94d64503565f75530e237dac6540e155\">c6f4922 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T01:36:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OpenBMB/ChatDev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ChatDev</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OpenBMB/ChatDev/tree/4fb2db0ea90375ce1059f44fe03ffbd191a7a169\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4fb2db0ea90375ce1059f44fe03ffbd191a7a169\">4fb2db0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: configured agents autonomously perform bounded task work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: the first-party majority-vote strategy provides a concrete composable conflict-resolution mechanism across multiple S1 outputs. It is not the default closed coordination policy and must be selected/configured by the parent, so it does not reach A.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: graph executors and node scheduling follow authored workflow structure; no autonomous superior regulator owns current whole-system priorities, resources or commitments.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: majority voting is peer-output reconciliation (S2), not an independent audit function. No distinct standard reviewer compares operational claims with evidence and returns corrective findings.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: workflow execution and task-local agent reasoning do not establish a prospective environment/intelligence loop for future organizational adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: roles, workflow topology, voting mode and ultimate task/policy boundary are parent-authored.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7da944be89535df1cb7917ad2d2fc20081578e30/assessments/chatdev.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7da944be89535df1cb7917ad2d2fc20081578e30\">7da944b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T21:22:10+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Yifan-Song793/RestGPT\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RestGPT</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Yifan-Song793/RestGPT/tree/d99012860cb45323975bf5780152d7aa44f390f5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d99012860cb45323975bf5780152d7aa44f390f5\">d990128 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-directed fulfillment of a user request by choosing, invoking and revising REST API operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, endpoint availability, API response content, partial progress and mismatch between the intermediate result and the user&#39;s requested outcome. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next natural-language plan, specific API endpoint/action/arguments and whether to continue or answer. - Decision owner: the LLM-based Planner, APISelector and Caller acting through the first-party session loop; the Python loop and API wrappers enforce or carry their choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OpenAPI endpoint descriptions, LangChain LLMChain/requests, OAuth or bearer credentials, response parser, history scratchpad and iteration limits. - Closure path: human query → model plan → selected API call → response and parsed facts → planner history → model continuation/revision or final answer. - Boundary reachability: run.py instantiates RestGPT with the model and selected spec and calls restgpt.run(query); call invokes Planner, APISelector and Caller in the released interactive mode, not solely in a benchmark. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model output selects and revises the operative task/API decisions; source-defined sequencing and API validation do not substitute their own discretionary plan. - Evidence: runner, agent loop, planner feedback, caller. - Basis: shipped autonomous session, not the paper&#39;s or README&#39;s agent label alone. - Confidence: high for code-path existence; effectiveness and safety untested. - Caveats: supported domains are TMDB/Spotify only; external model/API credentials and service access are required. The parser&#39;s Python exec is not sandboxed by the displayed code; this assessment is not an assurance of safe execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party regulation of interference between separate operational S1 units at the assessed session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential conflict among API calls or independently launched sessions is not accompanied by a shipped inter-S1 stabilization loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no inter-S1 arbitration or coordination discretion is established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planner → APISelector → Caller sequencing and shared history within one task. - Closure path: results return to the same operational agent, not to distinct S1 units through an attenuation relation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: specialized LLM components are roles of one agent session; their sequential messages are not proof of competing S1 work cells. - Evidence: single task loop, runner. - Basis: bounded negative within standard distribution. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: independent users could run separate processes, but the reviewed repository does not coordinate such sessions.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-session/current-operation control authority with a separate cross-S1 commitment or resource regulation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: excess iterations, wrong endpoint or failed API actions are handled as local S1 guard/retry and planning feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no whole-system allocation or intervention choice apart from task planning and fixed limits. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fixed maximum iterations/time, validator retry on unknown endpoint, chronological planner history and operator startup selection. - Closure path: bounded local task retries exist; no separate S3 current-control verdict changes resource/priority commitments of an operational whole. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model correction of its own task plan is S1 feedback; deterministic step ceilings do not own organizational resource decisions. - Evidence: iteration limits, guard and loop, selector retry. - Basis: scoped no-material-path conclusion for whole-system S3, despite operational guards. - Confidence: moderate-high. - Caveats: an application embedding this chain could supply S3 externally; it is not shipped here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no separate, independently controlled inspection of ordinary RestGPT action/result claims. - Disturbance / variety regulated: omitted API outcomes, unsupported claims or faulty parse results could require independent source checks, but none is wired as organizational complementary audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent audit verdict or accepted corrective decision is established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: response parsing, exception handling, logging and benchmark gold solution paths. - Closure path: parser summaries return through ordinary execution, not a complementary evidence channel to S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the same model-driven operational pipeline interprets responses; test datasets are adjacent evaluation material, not a separately operating auditor. - Evidence: response parser, benchmarks described in README. - Basis: absence of independent complementary access-and-return path. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: none of the reviewed source proves an independent audit performed on live runs.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no future-oriented environmental sensing, adaptation-option selection and return into RestGPT&#39;s standing capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external API evolution, new supported domains and future model limitations have no shipped prospective adaptation regulator. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S4 capability-change option decision in the operating session. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fixed bundled service specs, current query planner and research/benchmark documentation. - Closure path: task-level new facts alter the immediate API plan only, not durable future capability or present S3 policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: online planning adapts to a current task (S1); it does not maintain a future environment model or choose capability adaptations. - Evidence: runner&#39;s fixed domain selection, per-query planning. - Basis: bounded negative under supplied first-party runtime. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: maintainers can improve source and API specs outside this runtime; that development is not credited as its S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate-policy or organizational identity adjudication-and-return mechanism at this task-session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing claims about agent purpose, legitimate risk envelope and policy boundaries are not dynamically resolved by the program. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S5 identity/ultimate-policy decision path beyond static application design and startup parameters. - Decision owner: not established in the harness; humans choose the API scenario, supply credentials and state task instructions, but these alone are not a distinct S5 parent-governed circuit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fixed TMDB/Spotify scenario branches, static OpenAPI specs, supplied OAuth/bearer tokens and a user query. - Closure path: startup configuration controls which service is called, but no policy issue → legitimate authority decision → authoritative resolution → returned governance of subsequent activity is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: choosing credentials and launching a task is parent input and access setup, not evidence of a first-party, operationally closed identity-governance function. - Evidence: entry-point config and scenario selection, fixed scenario check. - Basis: functional/parent-mode threshold, not inferred from mere human ownership of setup. - Confidence: moderate-high. - Caveats: a surrounding institution may own policy in a deployment; such external governance is outside the first-party session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/01f50cbf59c509abf487b604408fcf9e478705dd/assessments/re/restgpt.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 01f50cbf59c509abf487b604408fcf9e478705dd\">01f50cb &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T00:37:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OWD-AI/AutoAgents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AutoAgents</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OWD-AI/AutoAgents/tree/223ad991988d752c446d25a0381e647f6e71c92c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 223ad991988d752c446d25a0381e647f6e71c92c\">223ad99 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute the supplied task through first-party model-driven expert actions and tool/result feedback inside the generated Group. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguity in the current task step, accumulated prior outputs, search/tool observations, uncertainty over which available action/tool to use and whether more subwork is required. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive current action/tool and action input from the step context, then use returned results to continue or conclude the step. - Decision owner: the LLM-driven generated CustomAction / Group operational loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fixed Manager-produced plan, Group step iterator, Environment shared memory/history, parser/schema repair, search wrapper, filesystem writes, budget check and external model/search APIs. - Closure path: user task → Manager constructs Group/plan → Group reaches a step → generated model action chooses operational action/tool → first-party runtime executes or records result → result is accumulated into later model iterations/plan steps → final task output. - Boundary reachability: startup.py creates the Manager and Environment.publishmessage creates the Group in the ordinary command-line/service path; Group.act directly runs the generated CustomAction instances, so the credited operational loop is wired into the shipped standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic plan ordering and runtime wrappers constrain execution, but the model chooses substantive per-step actions/tool inputs from current context. Removing the model decision leaves the Group without materially the same operational choice. - Evidence: startup.py, autoagents/environment.py, autoagents/roles/group.py, autoagents/actions/customaction.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the generated expert personas are implemented as action objects within a single Group role at this revision; S1=A therefore credits the autonomous operational cell without treating every persona label as a separate viable S1 recursion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no qualifying S2-specific attenuation of a concrete interference/conflict/oscillation among distinct S1 operational units is established in the shipped runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no specific qualifying inter-S1 disturbance is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2-specific coordination. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Manager-generated role division, fixed multi-step plan, shared Environment memory, Group accumulation of prior outputs and sequential invocation of generated expert actions. - Closure path: no evidenced inter-S1 disturbance → attenuation decision/relation → changed subsequent S1 behaviour loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the framework decomposes and sequences work, but the reviewed standard implementation collapses generated expert personas into action objects inside one Group role and does not identify a concrete cross-S1 conflict that its sequencing is specifically regulating. - Evidence: autoagents/environment.py, autoagents/roles/group.py, autoagents/roles/manager.py. - Basis: structural absence after function-first review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: older/commented per-role wiring or a downstream composition could create distinct S1 units and an S2 problem, but that is not the credited standard-distribution path at the pinned revision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no first-party whole-system current-control loop is established after the generated organization enters operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Manager handles task design variety before Group execution, but no actor is shown regulating changing whole-organization current commitments/resources/priorities while operations proceed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established over live whole-system operations. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Manager role/plan construction, Explorer budget limit, Environment scheduler/run loop, Group fixed step ordering and bounded action iterations. - Closure path: Manager design output creates the Group and plan, but once Group execution begins the Manager is excluded from the new-role execution loop; no live fleet/organization view → current-control decision → returned operational intervention path is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Manager owns decomposition/design choices, not evidenced current S3 regulation; deterministic budget/run/step machinery enforces bounds without owning whole-system current discretion. - Evidence: startup.py, autoagents/roles/manager.py, autoagents/environment.py, autoagents/roles/group.py. - Basis: structural absence after function-first review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the component is named Manager and creates the organization, but Profile 0.2.3 explicitly does not equate planning/task allocation with S3 without a whole-system current view and intervention right.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no reachable standard-distribution complementary audit path with sufficiently independent access to operational reality and corrective return is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: role/plan design errors are reviewed before execution, but hidden divergence between ordinary operational claims and independent reality is not audited through a qualifying S3 path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for a complete complementary operational audit loop. - Decision owner: none established for S3 publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Manager-owned CheckRoles / CheckPlans; repository ObserverAgents, ObserverPlans, and ActionObserver classes; Environment history/logging. - Closure path: Manager-generated role/plan artifacts → Manager&#39;s own review actions → suggestions → regenerated/reviewed design. This is pre-operation self-review. The separate Observer classes are not hired by startup.py, and the older ActionObserver wiring is commented out, so no standard complementary operational evidence → findings → corrective S1 return closes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-based review exists, but the active checks operate inside the same Manager planning path over its own design artifacts. They neither provide complementary access to live operational reality nor constitute a wired independent audit actor in the assessed mode. - Evidence: autoagents/roles/manager.py, autoagents/actions/checkroles.py, autoagents/actions/checkplans.py, startup.py, autoagents/environment.py, autoagents/roles/observer.py. - Basis: structural absence after boundary/reachability review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository-local Observer implementations show an intended audit-like design surface, but Methodology 0.3.5 forbids borrowing closure from adjacent/unwired first-party code.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop for future AutoAgents capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current task requirements and tool observations affect current execution, but prospective environmental change affecting future harness capability is not regulated through S4. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for selecting and promoting a future capability adaptation. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dynamic role/plan generation, role/action banks, search observations, memory/long-term-memory substrate and repeated Manager design review. - Closure path: user task → roles/plan tailored to that same task → current Group execution. No prospective environmental distinction → adaptation options → selected option → changed later AutoAgents capability/S3 loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Manager autonomously constructs a current-task organization, but Profile 0.2.3 distinguishes internal task planning from S4. RoleBank is a bundled/developer-authored capability surface rather than evidence of autonomous future adaptation. - Evidence: autoagents/roles/manager.py, autoagents/environment.py, autoagents/roles/rolebank/, autoagents/roles/role.py, README.md. - Basis: explicit/structural absence after future-adaptation review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: dynamic agent generation can be a mechanism used by an S4-capable downstream organization, but current-task staffing alone does not establish the S4 function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the assessed AutoAgents organization boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary task goals, provider/tool configuration and budget constrain work, but no identity-level S3–S4 tension or ultimate-policy issue is shown entering an authoritative closure loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5 publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user task, Manager prompt/goal, configured API/model/search choices, Explorer investment/budget and developer-authored role/action definitions. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority → authoritative decision → returned governance of subsequent operation path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Manager and generated roles operate under authored goals/constraints, but prompts/configuration and ordinary task authority are not S5 by themselves. - Evidence: startup.py, autoagents/explorer.py, autoagents/roles/manager.py, README.md. - Basis: structural absence after identity/policy review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the operator remains able to choose tasks/configuration, but generic operator input does not establish a first-party parent S5 mode without an identity-level issue and closed return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/21a05f691ca36fb20b98ea0c591738f8d2a3747a/assessments/autoagents.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 21a05f691ca36fb20b98ea0c591738f8d2a3747a\">21a05f6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T00:23:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aorwall/moatless-tools\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moatless Tools</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-22\">2026-10-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aorwall/moatless-tools/tree/011ead57a5c81664e9c45e07e1f50b17e695cc63\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 011ead57a5c81664e9c45e07e1f50b17e695cc63\">011ead5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: carry out a user-supplied coding task through model-selected actions on the workspace, with subsequent actions informed by returned observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain code location, incomplete task information, unsuccessful edits/tests, observed tool failures and alternative candidate task states. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ActionAgent.generateactions obtains model-created structured action arguments and chooses action types; ActionAgent.execute obtains the corresponding tool observation, used by later message generation and action steps. - Decision owner: configured model-driven ActionAgent in the reachable basic, iterative and tree-search task modes; flow and tool classes implement selection/dispatch rules and execution support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: action schema registry, completion model, message history, node/file context and artifact observations, flow loop, optional reward and feedback generators, workspace/runtime. - Closure path: task/root node → generated messages → model-selected action → tool execution → observation/artifact change attached to node → successor or selected task node → subsequent agent decision. - Boundary reachability: the README directly instantiates ActionAgent with an action and calls runsimple; AgenticFlow holds an ActionAgent and AgenticLoop / SearchTree call its run on reachable nodes in installed first-party code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model picks executable actions and their parameters from task context rather than receiving a fixed precomputed sequence; runner and action handlers simply execute/return observations. - Evidence: README direct example, agent model-action loop, iterative use. - Basis: direct first-party code and documented reachable example. - Confidence: high for supported S1 decision ownership; no claim about solution quality or benchmark success. - Caveats: the model provider/benchmark were not exercised; shadowmode and workspace setup affect persistence, not the existence of the task-local model/action choice.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: attenuate specific actual or structurally evidenced interference/oscillation between distinct operational S1 units and feed that correction into their subsequent work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing code edits across workers are possible externally, but the shipped tree branches are isolated candidate states under one action-agent execution path rather than distinct operational actors with conflicting active commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no inter-S1 collision detector and coordinating correction owner is evidenced in supported flows. - Decision owner: none at the assessed recursion; SearchTree selector/expander and scheduler choose candidate execution order according to configured rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: node parent/child links and cloned file context, optional reward/backpropagation and feedback, scheduler job caps and eventbus. - Closure path: candidate node → reward/feedback → subsequent candidate choice; this is optimization/feedback within S1 task search, not detection and damping of interference among independent S1 cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: selecting a candidate branch and avoiding duplicates does not resolve contradictory worker commitments; the shipped simple selector is deterministic and no separate inter-agent corrective feedback exists. - Evidence: SearchTree selection/expansion, child state expander, simple selector. - Basis: bounded negative from configured runtime architecture. - Confidence: high that no S2-specific inter-operational conflict relation is shipped in the reviewed flow modes. - Caveats: parallel SWE-bench jobs or third-party agents are different trajectories and external orchestration, not qualifying coordination by mere coexistence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: use a whole-current organization view to decide shared priorities, resources, commitments, accountability and corrective interventions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: too many search iterations, API jobs and candidate costs; these are controlled by preset caps or task-local optimizer logic, not evidenced discretionary present-wide governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: maxiterations, maxcost, branch count and scheduler concurrency caps are configuration/enforcement; no first-party agent or legitimate parent uses a whole-current view to trade off current operational commitments. - Decision owner: operators configure flows/models and optional job limits; code enforces them mechanically. No qualifying closed S3 decision owner in the standard task mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: FlowManager, trajectory metadata and storage, SchedulerRunner, AgenticFlow.isfinished, SearchTree cost/terminal criteria and UI requests. - Closure path: static limits/status → admit or stop task execution; missing sensed whole-current system dynamics → priority/resource/accountability decision → corrective return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model&#39;s per-node code action or a UCT/reward search policy is not present-wide organizational management; a scheduler enforcing a threshold does not own management just because it affects resource usage. - Evidence: flow constraints, search termination/backpropagation, scheduler caps, flow manager configuration. - Basis: bounded first-party negative; parent/operator modes checked explicitly. - Confidence: medium-high within the declared one-trajectory recursion. - Caveats: a human operating an external multi-project fleet may make actual S3 decisions, but that parent organization/return loop is not implemented by these configuration forms.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: independently inspect S3 operating/management claims via a complementary reality path and return corrective audit judgment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: erroneous generated edits and misleading task success/reward signals; the shipped validators concern candidate coding outcomes rather than independently assessing management commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent management auditor or S3-directed audit verdict path is wired in standard flows. - Decision owner: optional deterministic test/value functions or user-supplied feedback can evaluate task results, not management decisions at the chosen recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BaseValueFunction, optional BaseFeedbackGenerator, Maven compiler checker, node rewards and event logs. - Closure path: candidate code → compiler/test/reward feedback → another task search step; no complementary inspection of S3 claims → independent judgment → management correction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: even when an outcome validator runs separately from code generation, its subject is local code correctness, not S3 operating reality. Outcome reward parsing is not S3 ownership. - Evidence: value function contract, feedback injection, Maven compilation feedback. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: high for the reviewed published management-audit boundary. - Caveats: benchmark result verification and human review are adjacent/outside the target task&#39;s S3 organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: detect prospective external environment changes, form strategic capability options and adopt/return an option to present work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changed future models, technology, codebases and work requirements; the implemented code index, search and local refinement concern the present assigned problem. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no supported running component chooses enduring capability changes from prospective external sensing. - Decision owner: operator/maintainer selects flow and model configurations; ActionAgent chooses present-task actions; no autonomous or parent-closed S4 path is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configurable models/flows, branch rewards, history/context retrieval and benchmark research artifacts. - Closure path: source context → present task action or branch refinement; absent outside-future scan → options/adoption authority → persistent capability change affecting future operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: iterative tree search and reinforcement of candidate coding steps are not prospective strategy. Repository experiments and model choices do not prove an S4 loop in the deployed flow. - Evidence: README scopes/modes, flow model override, search reward propagation. - Basis: bounded first-party absence after checking plausible learning/search paths. - Confidence: high for absence in the reviewed task runtime. - Caveats: a future version or external research system might introduce S4; the frozen code does not.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: legitimately settle ultimate purpose/identity conflicts and return binding policy changes to system operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: mismatched user requests, model actions and code-project rules; there is no identity-policy adjudication protocol in the shipped flow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user input, agent system prompt and flow settings define the immediate task; neither model nor operator-facing runtime shows an explicit ultimate-policy dispute and authoritative ratification path. - Decision owner: users/config authors set initial goals and constraints, developers maintain the package; no qualifying closed runtime S5 mode is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: systemprompt, typed action schemas, model/flow config storage, operator project/trajectory controls and fixed resource restrictions. - Closure path: supplied task/prompt/config → coding actions; missing exceptional identity-policy escalation → legitimate resolution → revised binding governing identity in subsequent operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model chooses an action inside its supplied purpose and cannot be credited with legitimate revision of that purpose. Generic user configuration is not S5=P without the S5-specific policy loop. - Evidence: agent prompt and action schema, FlowManager configuration, README user-supplied goal. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high under the installed framework boundary. - Caveats: OSS maintainer governance or an embedding application&#39;s human policy authority may exist independently, but its policy-decision closure is not wired as a first-party S5 mode here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2bb5c0cae86593fceb5aa6a7126ecf7a3010dd6d/assessments/mo/moatless-tools.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2bb5c0cae86593fceb5aa6a7126ecf7a3010dd6d\">2bb5c0c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T16:29:23+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LangGraph</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/tree/e539ac122f4126f6dd850581c1494948cf620e31\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e539ac122f4126f6dd850581c1494948cf620e31\">e539ac1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: agent nodes can own model/tool action loops against durable state.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: the standard graph/runtime primitives do not specifically establish autonomous anti-oscillation or shared-resource regulation among two or more S1 units. A developer can author such a graph, but generic routing is not enough for C.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: no first-party autonomous whole-system regulator with current resource, priority or commitment authority is supplied at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: interrupts, checkpoint inspection, tracing hooks and resume are execution/debug/HITL mechanisms on the operational path. No distinct first-party reviewer independently compares operational claims with evidence and feeds corrective findings back into the system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: planning, persistence and arbitrary graph composition do not establish a prospective external-intelligence function coupled back to S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: interrupts return authority to a parent/human and application configuration remains parent-owned; the runtime does not close mission, identity or ultimate policy.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/fc807d03ef246e72491cb90117eb717c4a13ad9b/assessments/langgraph.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit fc807d03ef246e72491cb90117eb717c4a13ad9b\">fc807d0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T02:14:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/project-oagents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Project OAgents</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/project-oagents/tree/e4d262386860fbc586fe9af5fb13c0e293bf7b33\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e4d262386860fbc586fe9af5fb13c0e293bf7b33\">e4d2623 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Respond to user/environment events through model-authored content or intent classification and return model results into visible task outputs, follow-on agent events or next task revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unstructured user support, content-generation, requirements and code tasks; fresh model outputs, semantic memory results, optional auditor suggestions and domain event messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Supported runnable first-party examples call actual Semantic Kernel inference through base AiAgent&lt;T&gt;.CallFunction; model output is used as a Writer article, intent for Dispatcher routing or generated code/plan, and event delivery exposes the result to later actors. - Decision owner: Configured model-bearing AI agent subclass in the concrete example operating mode. Model supplies discretionary text/intent; hard-coded app role routing and external prompt, SK provider and developer permissions bound the operating choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Orleans/Dapr event bus and actor activation, persistent per-agent chat context/state, SK Kernel.InvokeAsync, supplied prompts, AddKnowledge memory lookup and demo event-processing handlers. - Closure path: Input UserChatInput / domain event → actor HandleEvent → first-party CallFunction and model response → ArticleCreated/support dispatch/GitHub-task output → event bus to consumer or visible UI. In marketing demo, a later AuditorAlert can trigger Writer revision using observed critique. - Boundary reachability: src/ AiAgent constructor and CallFunction are concrete inherited implementation; samples/marketing has a documented runnable backend/Orleans configuration and a concrete Writer : AiAgent&lt;WriterState&gt; subscribing to user events, calling SK and publishing downstream work. The basic abstract Agent on its own is only an extensible runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: At runnable sample boundary the configured model meaningfully chooses generated content, wording and intent from input rather than simply executing a hard-coded response; event transport, prompt choice and infrastructure are not credited autonomous policy. - Evidence: src/Oagents.Orleans/Agent.cs; src/Oagents.Orleans/AiAgent.cs; src/Oagents.Dapr/AiAgent.cs; samples/marketing/src/backend/Agents/Writer/Writer.cs; samples/marketing/src/backend/Agents/Writer/Writer.cs; samples/support-center/src/backend/Agents/Dispatcher/Dispatcher.cs; samples/gh-flow/src/Microsoft.AI.DevTeam/Agents/Developer/Developer.cs; samples/marketing/README.md. - Basis: First-party concrete model/function invocation and executable demo callback/result path; positive strictly scoped to the instantiated example agent rather than bare abstract interfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high for code reachability; low for untested hosted provider/actual model reliability. - Caveats: Model-generated output/intent qualifies limited S1 operational choice, not unrestricted autonomous tool selection. src/ alone cannot operate without a subclass, configured Semantic Kernel, credentials and host; examples are explicitly demonstrations and not evidence of production governance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No shipped first-party S2-specific control attenuates a demonstrable conflict or oscillation between independently viable S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple role agents can publish and receive events; duplicate/conflicting or competing work could occur, but no packaged active inter-unit disturbance regulator is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Event streams route events to actor subscriptions; support Dispatcher classifies which role receives a user request. Neither adjudicates concrete interference among distinct viable operating units or adjusts both sides to stabilize a shared constraint. - Decision owner: External developer scripts event routing and the sample-specific model intent classifier; no enduring S2 actor with specific damping mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Orleans stream ID/namespace subscriptions, Dapr PublishEvent, Dispatcher DispatcherChoice, typed events, static routing and application-written handlers. - Closure path: User event → Dispatcher or fixed stream → receiving agent; there is no sensed inter-agent disturbance → specific stabilization decision → feedback changing affected separate S1 agents&#39; operating behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An event bus, multi-role demo and model-chosen specialist routing are communication/dispatch, not automatically S2 coordination in the VSM sense. - Evidence: src/Oagents.Orleans/Agent.cs; src/Oagents.Orleans/Agent.cs; src/Oagents.Dapr/Agent.cs; samples/support-center/src/backend/Agents/Dispatcher/Dispatcher.cs; samples/support-center/src/backend/Agents/Dispatcher/Dispatcher.cs; samples/marketing/src/backend/Agents/Writer/Writer.cs. - Basis: Bounded absence after testing multi-agent buses, dispatcher, alternate backend and sample role composition. - Confidence: Medium-high within frozen first-party scope. - Caveats: Users could write an S2-specific event-policy agent; this extension possibility is not evidence the framework already owns S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No coherent system-wide current-control role with a whole-application view and binding priority/resource/commitment interventions across independently operating units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel marketing/support/developer requests, budget/capacity tradeoffs, deadlines and conflicting team commitments are possible; actual app-wide control over them is not implemented. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Sample Dispatcher selects a specialist based on a user message, DeveloperLead creates a task plan, and marketing actors publish outputs. No first-party system-level manager decides ongoing resource, responsibility or workstream priority across viable S1 cells. - Decision owner: Sample app developer, local business actor and event bus; model task plan and subscriber scheduling do not create a separate system-wide S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persisted actor histories, stream event delivery, per-role prompts, local coordination via events, optional Azure/Dapr hosting configuration. - Closure path: Role-specific current outputs and requests advance between actors; missing whole-current operating view → authoritative allocation/commitment decision → returned changed assignments across multiple S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Support dispatch, DevLead&#39;s task-specific planning and article workflow progression are S1 task decomposition. Neither host infrastructure nor a human authoring an example application supplies a demonstrated parent S3. - Evidence: samples/support-center/src/backend/Agents/Dispatcher/Dispatcher.cs; samples/support-center/src/backend/Agents/Dispatcher/Dispatcher.cs; samples/gh-flow/src/Microsoft.AI.DevTeam/Agents/DeveloperLead/DeveloperLead.cs; samples/gh-flow/src/Microsoft.AI.DevTeam/Agents/Developer/Developer.cs; samples/marketing/src/backend/Agents/Writer/Writer.cs; src/Oagents.Orleans/Agent.cs. - Basis: Tested plausible manager candidates at actual application recursion, bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high for package and supported examples. - Caveats: A real enterprise organization can have parent S3 outside this framework; not inherited from Microsoft&#39;s developer project.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independently governed whole-current complementary audit challenges ordinary S3 management claims and authoritatively returns corrective control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Marketing copy may breach a ten-percent discount constraint; an in-tree demo Auditor inspects generated article text and publishes a targeted corrective alert. No independent whole-current reality-check mechanism is packaged for framework control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Sample Auditor has a model-based text-specific rule and can send AuditorAlert to Writer, which re-generates text. That is a genuine scoped first-party demonstration of QA feedback, not an owner with independent direct access to operating facts against S3 reporting and a returned S3 corrective mandate. - Decision owner: Marketing demo Auditor model and Writer request handler; the prompt policy is hard-coded in sample. There is no standalone S3 role in reusable src/ and no shown ordinary S3 system whose claims can be audited. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AuditText and AuditorAlert events, sample model prompt, article text relay, Writer Adjust prompt, UI SignalR alert. - Closure path: Writer generates text → publishes AuditText → demo Auditor checks discount text → emits AuditorAlert → Writer adjusts text. This task-local revision loop exists, but there is no separate independently sourced operational audit of a current system-control claim nor a return to a legitimate S3 decision affecting actual S1 commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent agent identity and second opinion about generated content do not suffice for VSM S3 when it receives the same produced output, judges only a hard-coded local copy constraint and lacks a complementary direct-evidence path/organizational controller. - Evidence: samples/marketing/src/backend/Agents/Writer/Writer.cs; samples/marketing/src/backend/Agents/Writer/Writer.cs; samples/marketing/src/backend/Agents/Writer/Writer.cs; samples/marketing/src/backend/Agents/Auditor/Auditor.cs; samples/marketing/src/backend/Agents/Auditor/Auditor.cs; samples/marketing/src/backend/Agents/Auditor/Auditor.cs; samples/marketing/src/backend/Agents/Auditor/AuditorPrompts.cs; samples/marketing/README.md. - Basis: Strongest positive candidate (first-party Writer/Auditor corrective loop) inspected rather than dismissed by filename; bounded to demo QA rather than organizational complementary audit. - Confidence: Medium; distinction depends on fixed application recursion and complementary-access requirement. - Caveats: The demo proves writer/auditor task correction, not real independent S3 governance. Re-scoping to an organization with independent operating evidence and a genuine S3 actor would be a separate source-backed case.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party prospective intelligence unit selects future-environment options and authorizes changed organizational capabilities in the running system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Market, customer or software-development needs could change; semantic memory lookup can inject existing knowledge for present task generation, not govern future adaptations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: AddKnowledge queries semantic memory and fills prompt arguments; sample authors choose templates and actor topology. No code path independently evaluates longer-horizon alternatives and installs accepted process/tool/organizational changes. - Decision owner: Application developer and local role-specific LLM task generator, not a future-intelligence authority with capability-change feedback return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Semantic Kernel memory search, prompt templates, persistent chat state, events and provider configuration, demonstration planners. - Closure path: Task arrives → existing memory/documents incorporated into current prompt → result; missing outside-future sensing → strategic options → authorized future capability transformation → subsequent operating adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Knowledge retrieval for current inference and generating a development plan for a ticket are not automatic S4 future viability adaptation. - Evidence: src/Oagents.Orleans/AiAgent.cs; src/Oagents.Dapr/AiAgent.cs; samples/gh-flow/src/Microsoft.AI.DevTeam/Agents/DeveloperLead/DeveloperLead.cs; samples/gh-flow/src/Microsoft.AI.DevTeam/Agents/Developer/Developer.cs; README.md. - Basis: Bounded negative after testing retrieval and sample future-looking planning as positive candidates. - Confidence: Medium-high at frozen application recursion. - Caveats: Custom app agents may create R&amp;D or change management workflows beyond the shipped core; not imported as S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No built-in legitimate ultimate purpose/identity authority adjudicates competing goals and returns governing organizational policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Hard-coded marketing discount policy and support routing choices constrain local outputs; they do not constitute identity-level arbitration of a viable organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer sets Namespace, event type, prompts and metadata; sample Auditor applies a discount ceiling, not final ultimate mission authority. External Microsoft CLA and code licenses concern OSS authorship rather than runtime S5. - Decision owner: Sample author/operator, externally configured business and provider environment; no first-party ultimate purpose-governance actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Fixed agent role classes, sample marketing audit prompt, stream namespace, configurable service settings, repository code/documentation licenses. - Closure path: Local marketing content constraint → alert/rewrite on a task; missing values/legitimacy dispute → S5 final binding identity decision → corresponding whole-system policy return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Conformance to one threshold, task routing or owner-supplied initial identity is narrower than deciding what the whole system ultimately is/ought to become. - Evidence: samples/marketing/src/backend/Agents/Auditor/AuditorPrompts.cs; src/Oagents.Orleans/Agent.cs; src/Oagents.Dapr/Agent.cs; README.md. - Basis: First-party local policy and identity paths inspected, bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high for package-as-shipped and demo scope. - Caveats: Actual adopting enterprise may legally own S5; that external authority is not a first-party runtime path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e2048393f6108c33f20d2716c31f59ecc18b3fc4/assessments/project-oagents.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e2048393f6108c33f20d2716c31f59ecc18b3fc4\">e204839 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T22:21:05Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openinterpreter/openinterpreter\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open Interpreter</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openinterpreter/openinterpreter/tree/f110a7a85f43efc343f936f5bc504e01eb262550\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f110a7a85f43efc343f936f5bc504e01eb262550\">f110a7a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: the coding agent autonomously inspects/edits the workspace, executes tools and reacts to results.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: stable first-party MultiAgentV2 exposes ongoing messaging, follow-up work, waits/interrupts and team-state listing over a shared workspace, enough to compose mutual-adjustment/collision-regulation. Because the richer path is default-disabled and coordination policy is not closed by the runtime, it is not A.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: agent-control and permission services can list, spawn, interrupt and constrain work, but the harness does not give an autonomous agent a whole-system current view plus authority over shared budgets/priorities/commitments as an S3 function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: Guardian supplies a distinct reviewer session that examines a proposed privileged action against contextual evidence and can deny it or trigger interruption, materially differing from the operating agent&#39;s normal production path. Reviewer/approval configuration determines when it is active, so it is composable rather than unconditional agent-owned audit.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: web/tools, memory and goals do not establish a distinct external-and-prospective adaptation function in two-way conversation with S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: AGENTS.md, permission profiles, approval policy, hooks and harness configuration are user/runtime/managed constraints; no agent-owned ultimate identity/policy closure is established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ae8749aa005ea42801ed86e32476f5c417ecd9a0/assessments/open-interpreter.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ae8749aa005ea42801ed86e32476f5c417ecd9a0\">ae8749a &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
